ASSIGN.push(
{id:"hw4", title:"Homework 4 · Lorentz force, forces on wires, torque, Biot–Savart", icon:"4️⃣", c:["#8b5cf6","#ec4899"], note:"Lecture 7. Constants: μ₀ = 4π×10⁻⁷ T·m/A, e = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C, mₑ = 9.11×10⁻³¹ kg, 1 u = 1.66×10⁻²⁷ kg.", items:[
 {pid:"a4:0", src:"HW4 Q1 · velocity selector & mass spectrometer", q:R`A beam of singly ionized argon ions ($^{40}$Ar$^+$, mass $m=40$ u $=6.64\times10^{-26}$ kg, charge $+e$) travels in the $+x$ direction and enters a velocity selector in which a uniform electric field $\vec E=1.5\times10^5\,\hat y$ V/m and a uniform magnetic field $\vec B_1$ of magnitude 0.30 T are applied, as shown in Figure Q1.<br>(a) In which direction must $\vec B_1$ point so that the ions can pass through the selector undeflected? Find the speed $v$ of the ions that emerge from the selector.<br>(b) The emerging ions enter a region of uniform magnetic field $\vec B_2=0.50\,\hat z$ T (no electric field) and follow a semicircular path to a detector. Find the radius $r$ of the path and the time the ions spend in the field. (Hint: for uniform circular motion, the net inward force has magnitude $F_c=mv^2/r$.)<br>(c) The beam also contains $^{36}$Ar$^+$ ions ($m=36$ u) moving at the same speed. How far apart are the $^{40}$Ar$^+$ and $^{36}$Ar$^+$ impact points on the detector?`,
  a:S(R`In the selector the electric and magnetic forces must cancel, $q\vec E+q\vec v\times\vec B_1=0$. In $\vec B_2$ the magnetic force is the centripetal force, $qvB=mv^2/r$.`,[
   R`<b>(a) Direction.</b> The electric force on the positive ion is $q\vec E=+eE\,\hat y$. The magnetic force must be $-eE\,\hat y$. With $\vec v=v\hat x$: $\hat x\times\hat z=-\hat y$, so $\vec B_1$ must point along $+\hat z$ (out of the page): $\vec B_1=0.30\,\hat z$ T.`,
   R`<b>(a) Speed.</b> Balance $eE=evB_1$ ⇒ $v=\dfrac{E}{B_1}=\dfrac{1.5\times10^5}{0.30}=5.0\times10^5$ m/s. Only ions with exactly this speed go straight through.`,
   R`<b>(b) Radius.</b> $r=\dfrac{mv}{eB_2}=\dfrac{(6.64\times10^{-26})(5.0\times10^5)}{(1.602\times10^{-19})(0.50)}=\dfrac{3.32\times10^{-20}}{8.01\times10^{-20}}=0.414$ m.`,
   R`<b>(b) Time.</b> A semicircle is half a period: $t=\dfrac{\pi r}{v}=\dfrac{\pi m}{eB_2}=\dfrac{\pi(0.414)}{5.0\times10^5}=2.60\times10^{-6}$ s. (The period $2\pi m/qB$ does not depend on the speed.)`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> $m_{36}=36(1.66\times10^{-27})=5.98\times10^{-26}$ kg, same $v$: $r_{36}=\dfrac{(5.98\times10^{-26})(5.0\times10^5)}{8.01\times10^{-20}}=0.373$ m. Each ion lands one <i>diameter</i> from the entry slit, so the separation is $2(r_{40}-r_{36})=2(0.414-0.373)=0.083$ m.`],
   R`(a) $\vec B_1=+0.30\,\hat z$ T (out of page); $v=\mathbf{5.0\times10^5}$ m/s. (b) $r=\mathbf{0.41}$ m, $t=\mathbf{2.6\ \mu s}$. (c) $\Delta x=2\Delta r\approx\mathbf{8.3}$ <b>cm</b>.`,
   R`The impact points are separated by the difference in <i>diameters</i>, not radii.`)},
 {pid:"a4:1", src:"HW4 Q2 · Hall effect", q:R`A flat copper strip of width $w=2.0$ cm (along $y$) and thickness $t=1.0$ mm (along $z$) carries a current $I=20$ A in the $+x$ direction. A uniform magnetic field $\vec B=1.5\,\hat z$ T is applied perpendicular to the face of the strip (Figure Q2). The free-electron density of copper is $n=8.5\times10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$.<br>(a) Find the drift speed $v_d$ of the conduction electrons. (Recall $\vec J=\rho\vec v$ from Lecture 6.)<br>(b) The magnetic force deflects the electrons toward one edge of the strip, where charge accumulates until the resulting transverse electric field $\vec E_H$ exactly cancels the magnetic force on the carriers — the same balance as in a velocity selector. Find the magnitude and direction of $\vec E_H$ and the Hall voltage $V_H$ between the two edges, defined by $V_H=V(+w/2)-V(-w/2)$.<br>(c) Which edge, $y=+w/2$ or $y=-w/2$, is at the higher potential? Would your answer change if the current were carried by positive charges instead? (This is the Hall effect.)`,
  a:S(R`Drift speed from $J=nev_d$. In steady state the Hall field balances the magnetic force: $eE_H=ev_dB$.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $J=\dfrac{I}{wt}=\dfrac{20}{(0.020)(1.0\times10^{-3})}=1.0\times10^6$ A/m². $v_d=\dfrac{J}{ne}=\dfrac{1.0\times10^6}{(8.5\times10^{28})(1.602\times10^{-19})}=7.3\times10^{-5}$ m/s. The electrons move along $-\hat x$ (opposite to $I$).`,
   R`<b>(b) Which way are electrons pushed?</b> $\vec F=q\vec v\times\vec B=(-e)(-v_d\hat x)\times(B\hat z)=ev_dB(\hat x\times\hat z)=-ev_dB\,\hat y$. Electrons pile up on the $y=-w/2$ edge, leaving the $+w/2$ edge positive.`,
   R`<b>Hall field.</b> It points from the positive edge to the negative edge: $\vec E_H=-E_H\hat y$, with $E_H=v_dB=(7.3\times10^{-5})(1.5)=1.1\times10^{-4}$ V/m. Check: force on an electron $-e\vec E_H=+eE_H\hat y$, which cancels the magnetic force ✓.`,
   R`<b>Hall voltage.</b> $V_H=V(+w/2)-V(-w/2)=-\displaystyle\int_{-w/2}^{w/2}E_y\,dy=E_Hw=(1.1\times10^{-4})(0.020)=2.2\times10^{-6}$ V. (Equivalently $V_H=\dfrac{IB}{net}$.)`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> The $y=+w/2$ edge is higher. For positive carriers moving along $+x$, $\vec F=e(v\hat x)\times(B\hat z)=-evB\hat y$ as well, so <i>positive</i> charge piles up at $-w/2$: the sign of $V_H$ flips. Measuring the sign of $V_H$ tells you the sign of the carriers.`],
   R`(a) $v_d=\mathbf{7.3\times10^{-5}}$ m/s. (b) $\vec E_H=-1.1\times10^{-4}\,\hat y$ V/m, $V_H=\mathbf{+2.2\ \mu V}$. (c) $y=+w/2$ is higher; with positive carriers it would be $y=-w/2$ (the answer changes).`)},
 {pid:"a4:2", src:"HW4 Q3 · force on a bent wire", q:R`A rigid wire carries a current $I=6.0$ A from point A$(0,0,0)$ to point C$(0.4,0.3,0)$ m along two straight segments, A → B$(0.4,0,0)$ m → C, in a uniform magnetic field $\vec B=0.50\,\hat z$ T (Figure Q3).<br>(a) Find the magnetic force on segment AB and on segment BC.<br>(b) Find the total force on the wire, and show that it equals the force on a single straight wire carrying the same current directly from A to C.<br>(c) The wire is now closed by adding a straight segment from C back to A, forming a triangular loop. What is the net force on the loop, and what is the torque on it?`,
  a:S(R`For a straight segment in a uniform field, $\vec F=I\vec L\times\vec B$, with $\vec L$ pointing along the current.`,[
   R`<b>(a) AB:</b> $\vec L=0.4\hat x$. $\vec F_{AB}=6(0.4\hat x)\times(0.5\hat z)=1.2(\hat x\times\hat z)=-1.2\,\hat y$ N.`,
   R`<b>BC:</b> $\vec L=0.3\hat y$. $\vec F_{BC}=6(0.3\hat y)\times(0.5\hat z)=0.9(\hat y\times\hat z)=+0.9\,\hat x$ N.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $\vec F=0.9\hat x-1.2\hat y$ N, $|\vec F|=\sqrt{0.81+1.44}=1.5$ N. Straight A→C: $\vec L=0.4\hat x+0.3\hat y$, $\vec F=6(0.4\hat x+0.3\hat y)\times0.5\hat z=3(-0.4\hat y+0.3\hat x)=0.9\hat x-1.2\hat y$ N — the same. In a uniform field only the start and end points matter, because $\int I\,d\vec l\times\vec B=I\big(\int d\vec l\big)\times\vec B$.`,
   R`<b>(c) Force.</b> C→A has $\vec L=-(0.4\hat x+0.3\hat y)$, giving $-0.9\hat x+1.2\hat y$ N. The net force on the closed loop is zero ($\oint d\vec l=0$).`,
   R`<b>(c) Torque.</b> The loop A→B→C→A runs counterclockwise seen from $+z$, so $\vec\mu=IA\hat z$ with $A=\tfrac12(0.4)(0.3)=0.06$ m²: $\vec\mu=0.36\,\hat z$ A·m². $\vec\tau=\vec\mu\times\vec B=0$ because $\vec\mu\parallel\vec B$.`],
   R`(a) $\vec F_{AB}=\mathbf{-1.2\,\hat y}$ N, $\vec F_{BC}=\mathbf{+0.9\,\hat x}$ N. (b) $\mathbf{0.9\hat x-1.2\hat y}$ N (1.5 N), same as the straight wire A→C. (c) Net force $\mathbf 0$, torque $\mathbf 0$ (the loop's moment is parallel to $\vec B$).`)},
 {pid:"a4:3", src:"HW4 Q4 · three parallel wires", q:R`Three long, straight, parallel wires pass through the corners of an equilateral triangle of side $d=10$ cm (Figure Q4). Wires 1 and 2 each carry $I=10$ A out of the page; wire 3 carries $I=10$ A into the page. (Hint: the field of a long straight wire is $B=\mu_0I/(2\pi r)$, derived in the example class.)<br>(a) Find the magnitude and direction of the magnetic force per unit length on wire 3.<br>(b) Find the magnitude and direction of the magnetic force per unit length on wire 1.<br>(c) What is the total force per unit length on the three-wire system? Explain your answer without detailed calculation.`,
  a:S(R`Two long parallel wires exert $f=\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}$ per metre on each other, attractive for parallel currents and repulsive for antiparallel currents. Add the pair forces as vectors. Coordinates: wire 1 at $(-d/2,0)$, wire 2 at $(d/2,0)$, wire 3 at $(0,\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}d)$.`,[
   R`<b>Pair force.</b> $f=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(10)(10)}{2\pi(0.10)}=2.0\times10^{-4}$ N/m for every pair.`,
   R`<b>(a) Wire 3</b> is antiparallel to both 1 and 2, so it is repelled by each, along 1→3 $=(\tfrac12,\tfrac{\sqrt3}2)$ and 2→3 $=(-\tfrac12,\tfrac{\sqrt3}2)$. The $x$ parts cancel: $\vec f_3=2f\cdot\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\,\hat y=\sqrt3 f\,\hat y=3.46\times10^{-4}\,\hat y$ N/m, straight away from the base (up the page).`,
   R`<b>(b) Wire 1</b> is attracted to wire 2 (parallel): $f\,\hat x$. It is repelled by wire 3 along 3→1 $=(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac{\sqrt3}2)$: $f(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac{\sqrt3}2)$. Sum: $\vec f_1=f\big(\tfrac12\hat x-\tfrac{\sqrt3}2\hat y\big)=1.0\times10^{-4}\hat x-1.73\times10^{-4}\hat y$ N/m. Magnitude $f=2.0\times10^{-4}$ N/m, pointing $60°$ below the $+x$ axis.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> By Newton's third law, wire $i$ pushes on wire $j$ exactly as hard as $j$ pushes on $i$, in the opposite direction. All the forces are internal pairs, so they cancel: total force per unit length $=0$. Check: $\vec f_2=f(-\tfrac12\hat x-\tfrac{\sqrt3}2\hat y)$ by symmetry, and $\vec f_1+\vec f_2+\vec f_3=0$ ✓.`],
   R`(a) $\mathbf{3.5\times10^{-4}}$ N/m, perpendicular to the line 1–2, away from it. (b) $\mathbf{2.0\times10^{-4}}$ N/m at $60°$ below $+x$ (toward wire 2 and away from wire 3). (c) $\mathbf 0$ — internal action–reaction pairs.`)},
 {pid:"a4:4", src:"HW4 Q5 · rectangle vs circle", q:R`(Note given in the homework: at the centre of a circular loop of radius $R$, $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2R}$; the field of a straight segment at perpendicular distance $r$ is $\vec B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{4\pi r}(\cos\theta_1-\cos\theta_2)\hat\phi$, where $\theta_1,\theta_2$ are the angles between the current direction and the lines joining the two ends of the segment to P (Figure A).)<br>A rectangular loop of wire with sides $a=30$ cm and $b=40$ cm carries a current $I=3.0$ A.<br>(a) Find the magnetic flux density $B$ at the centre of the rectangle.<br>(b) The same piece of wire (length 1.4 m) is now bent into a circle carrying the same current. Find $B$ at the centre of the circle and compare with part (a).`,
  a:S(R`Use the finite-segment formula for each side. At the centre every side gives a field in the same direction (normal to the loop), so the four magnitudes add. For a segment of length $L$ viewed from its perpendicular bisector at distance $r$: $\cos\theta_1-\cos\theta_2=\dfrac{L}{\sqrt{r^2+L^2/4}}$.`,[
   R`<b>Half-diagonal.</b> $\sqrt{0.15^2+0.20^2}=0.25$ m (the distance from the centre to each corner).`,
   R`<b>Sides of length $a=0.30$ m</b> are $r=b/2=0.20$ m away: $\cos\theta_1-\cos\theta_2=\dfrac{0.30}{0.25}=1.2$, so $B=\dfrac{10^{-7}(3)}{0.20}(1.2)=1.8\ \mu$T each, $3.6\ \mu$T for the pair.`,
   R`<b>Sides of length $b=0.40$ m</b> are $r=a/2=0.15$ m away: $\cos\theta_1-\cos\theta_2=\dfrac{0.40}{0.25}=1.6$, so $B=\dfrac{10^{-7}(3)}{0.15}(1.6)=3.2\ \mu$T each, $6.4\ \mu$T for the pair.`,
   R`<b>(a) Total</b> $B=3.6+6.4=10.0\ \mu$T, perpendicular to the plane of the loop. (General form: $B=\dfrac{2\mu_0I\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}{\pi ab}$.)`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $2\pi R=1.4$ ⇒ $R=0.223$ m. $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2R}=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(3.0)}{2(0.223)}=8.5\ \mu$T.`],
   R`(a) $B=\mathbf{10\ \mu T}$. (b) $B=\mathbf{8.5\ \mu T}$; the rectangle gives about 18% more, because parts of its wire pass closer to the centre (0.15 m) than the circle does (0.223 m).`)},
 {pid:"a4:5", src:"HW4 Q6 · Bohr atom as a current loop", q:R`In the Bohr model of the hydrogen atom, the electron (charge $-e$) moves around the proton in a circular orbit of radius $r=0.529\times10^{-10}$ m with speed $v=2.19\times10^6$ m/s (the same orbit as in the optional problems of HW 1).<br>(a) Treating the orbiting electron as a circular current loop, find the equivalent current $I$.<br>(b) Find the magnitude of the magnetic flux density $B$ at the proton (the centre of the orbit).<br>(c) Find the magnetic dipole moment $\mu$ of the orbit. (Your answer is the Bohr magneton, the natural unit of atomic magnetic moments.)<br>(d) Alternatively, treat the electron as a moving point charge and use $\vec B=\dfrac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\dfrac{q\vec v\times\hat r}{r^2}$ to find $B$ at the proton. Does it agree with part (b)?`,
  a:S(R`One electron passes any point once per period, so $I=e/T$. Then use the loop results $B=\mu_0I/2r$ and $\mu=I\pi r^2$.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $T=\dfrac{2\pi r}{v}=\dfrac{2\pi(0.529\times10^{-10})}{2.19\times10^6}=1.52\times10^{-16}$ s. $I=\dfrac eT=\dfrac{1.602\times10^{-19}}{1.52\times10^{-16}}=1.06\times10^{-3}$ A. (Conventional current flows opposite to the electron's motion.)`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2r}=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(1.06\times10^{-3})}{2(0.529\times10^{-10})}=12.5$ T. A huge field, because $r$ is tiny.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> $\mu=I\pi r^2=(1.06\times10^{-3})\pi(0.529\times10^{-10})^2=9.27\times10^{-24}$ A·m². Equivalent form: $\mu=\tfrac12evr$.`,
   R`<b>(d)</b> $\vec v\perp\hat r$ everywhere on the orbit, so $B=\dfrac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\dfrac{ev}{r^2}=10^{-7}\dfrac{(1.602\times10^{-19})(2.19\times10^6)}{(0.529\times10^{-10})^2}=12.5$ T. It agrees: substituting $I=ev/(2\pi r)$ into $\mu_0I/2r$ gives exactly $\dfrac{\mu_0ev}{4\pi r^2}$.`],
   R`(a) $I=\mathbf{1.06\ mA}$. (b) $B=\mathbf{12.5\ T}$. (c) $\mu=\mathbf{9.27\times10^{-24}\ A\cdot m^2}$ (the Bohr magneton). (d) $\mathbf{12.5\ T}$, the same.`)},
]},

{id:"hw4o", title:"Homework 4 · optional practice (with solutions)", icon:"🧲", c:["#a855f7","#6366f1"], note:"The supplied solutions, with the reasoning spelled out.", items:[
 {pid:"a4o:0", src:"HW4 Optional Q1 · helical motion", q:R`An electron ($m=9.11\times10^{-31}$ kg) moving with speed $v=3.0\times10^6$ m/s enters a uniform magnetic field $B=2.0$ mT. Its velocity makes an angle of 30° with $\vec B$.<br>(a) Resolve $\vec v$ into components parallel and perpendicular to $\vec B$, and describe the resulting motion.<br>(b) Find the radius of the circular part of the motion.<br>(c) Find the period of one revolution.<br>(d) Find the pitch of the helix (the distance the electron advances along $\vec B$ in one revolution).`,
  a:S(R`The magnetic force $q\vec v\times\vec B$ ignores $v_\parallel$ and bends $v_\perp$ into a circle: the path is a helix.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $v_\parallel=v\cos30°=2.60\times10^6$ m/s (unaffected by $\vec B$); $v_\perp=v\sin30°=1.50\times10^6$ m/s (uniform circular motion). Together: a helix whose axis is along $\vec B$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $ev_\perp B=\dfrac{mv_\perp^2}{r}$ ⇒ $r=\dfrac{mv_\perp}{eB}=\dfrac{(9.11\times10^{-31})(1.5\times10^6)}{(1.602\times10^{-19})(2.0\times10^{-3})}=4.3$ mm.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> $T=\dfrac{2\pi r}{v_\perp}=\dfrac{2\pi m}{eB}=\dfrac{2\pi(9.11\times10^{-31})}{(1.602\times10^{-19})(2.0\times10^{-3})}=1.79\times10^{-8}$ s $=17.9$ ns.`,
   R`<b>(d)</b> Pitch $p=v_\parallel T=(2.60\times10^6)(1.79\times10^{-8})=4.6\times10^{-2}$ m.`],
   R`(a) Helix about $\vec B$, $v_\parallel=2.6\times10^6$, $v_\perp=1.5\times10^6$ m/s. (b) $r=\mathbf{4.3\ mm}$. (c) $T=\mathbf{17.9\ ns}$. (d) $p=\mathbf{4.6\ cm}$.`)},
 {pid:"a4o:1", src:"HW4 Optional Q2 · loop next to a long wire", q:R`A long straight wire carrying $I_1=20$ A lies along the $y$ axis. A rectangular loop with sides $a=20$ cm (parallel to the wire) and $b=10$ cm lies in the $xy$-plane with its nearest side at distance $d=5.0$ cm from the wire; it carries $I_2=5.0$ A in the sense shown in Figure OQ2 (the current in the near side is parallel to $I_1$).<br>(a) Find the force on each of the four sides of the loop.<br>(b) Find the net force on the loop (magnitude and direction). Is the loop attracted to or repelled from the wire?`,
  a:S(R`The wire's field is not uniform, so the near and far sides feel different forces. With $I_1$ along $+\hat y$, the field for $x>0$ is $\vec B=-\dfrac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi x}\hat z$ (into the page).`,[
   R`<b>Near side</b> ($x=d$, current $+\hat y$): $\vec F=I_2a\,\hat y\times\Big(-\dfrac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi d}\hat z\Big)=-\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2a}{2\pi d}\hat x=-\dfrac{(2\times10^{-7})(20)(5)(0.20)}{0.05}\hat x=-8.0\times10^{-5}\hat x$ N.`,
   R`<b>Far side</b> ($x=d+b$, current $-\hat y$): $\vec F=+\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2a}{2\pi(d+b)}\hat x=+\dfrac{(2\times10^{-7})(20)(5)(0.20)}{0.15}\hat x=+2.67\times10^{-5}\hat x$ N.`,
   R`<b>Top and bottom sides</b> (currents $\pm\hat x$ from $x=d$ to $d+b$): $\vec F=I_2\displaystyle\int_d^{d+b}\hat x\times\Big(-\frac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi x}\hat z\Big)dx=+\hat y\,\frac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi}\ln\frac{d+b}{d}=\pm2.2\times10^{-5}\hat y$ N. They are equal and opposite.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $\vec F_{net}=(-8.0+2.67)\times10^{-5}\hat x=-5.3\times10^{-5}\hat x$ N, toward the wire. The near side (parallel current, attracts) is closer than the far side (antiparallel, repels), so attraction wins.`],
   R`(a) Near: $8.0\times10^{-5}$ N toward the wire; far: $2.7\times10^{-5}$ N away; top/bottom: $2.2\times10^{-5}$ N, opposite. (b) $\mathbf{5.3\times10^{-5}}$ <b>N toward the wire — attracted</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a4o:2", src:"HW4 Optional Q3 · Earth's dipole field", q:R`Earth's magnetic field is well approximated by that of a magnetic dipole of moment $\mu=8.0\times10^{22}$ A·m² at the centre of the Earth (radius $R_E=6.37\times10^6$ m), with the dipole axis roughly along the rotation axis. Use the far-field dipole expression from the lecture, $\vec B=\dfrac{\mu_0\mu}{4\pi R^3}(2\cos\theta\,\hat R+\sin\theta\,\hat\theta)$, where $\theta$ is measured from the dipole axis.<br>(a) Find the magnitude of $B$ at the surface at the magnetic equator ($\theta=90°$) and at the magnetic pole ($\theta=0$).<br>(b) At $\theta=45°$, find the angle between $\vec B$ and the local horizontal. State whether the field points above or below the horizontal.<br>(c) If the dipole moment were produced by a single circular current loop of radius $R_E$ around the equator, what current would be required?`,
  a:S(R`Evaluate the prefactor $B_0=\dfrac{\mu_0\mu}{4\pi R_E^3}$ once; the radial component is vertical and the $\hat\theta$ component is horizontal.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $B_0=\dfrac{10^{-7}(8.0\times10^{22})}{(6.37\times10^6)^3}=31\ \mu$T. Equator: only $\sin\theta\,\hat\theta$ survives ⇒ $B=B_0=31\ \mu$T. Pole: only $2\cos\theta\,\hat R$ ⇒ $B=2B_0=62\ \mu$T.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $B_R=2B_0\cos45°$ (vertical), $B_\theta=B_0\sin45°$ (horizontal). $\tan I=\dfrac{B_R}{B_\theta}=2\cot\theta=2$ ⇒ $I=63.4°$. Following the lecture's formula, $B_R>0$ points radially out, i.e. above the horizontal. $|B|=B_0\sqrt{1+3\cos^2\theta}=49\ \mu$T.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> $\mu=I\pi R_E^2$ ⇒ $I=\dfrac{8.0\times10^{22}}{\pi(6.37\times10^6)^2}=6.3\times10^8$ A.`],
   R`(a) $\mathbf{31\ \mu T}$ at the equator, $\mathbf{62\ \mu T}$ at the pole. (b) $\mathbf{63.4°}$ above the horizontal, $|B|=49\ \mu$T. (c) $I=\mathbf{6.3\times10^8}$ <b>A</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a4o:3", src:"HW4 Optional Q4 · torque on a rectangular loop", q:R`A rectangular loop with sides $a=10$ cm (along $x$) and $b=6.0$ cm (along $y$) lies in the $xy$-plane with one corner at the origin, and carries $I=3.0$ A counterclockwise when viewed from the $+z$ side. A uniform field $\vec B=0.20\,\hat x+0.40\,\hat z$ T is applied. See Figure OQ4.<br>(a) Find the magnetic dipole moment $\vec\mu$ of the loop.<br>(b) Find the torque $\vec\tau$ on the loop using $\vec\tau=\vec\mu\times\vec B$. About which axis does the loop tend to rotate, and in which sense?<br>(c) Find the force on each of the four sides, and verify that the net force is zero and that the sum of the torques $\vec r\times\vec F$ about the centre of the loop agrees with part (b).`,
  a:S(R`$\vec\mu=IA\hat n$ with $\hat n$ from the right-hand rule on the current; only the component of $\vec B$ perpendicular to $\vec\mu$ produces torque.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> Counterclockwise from $+z$ ⇒ $\hat n=+\hat z$. $\vec\mu=IA\hat n=3.0(0.10\times0.060)\hat z=0.018\,\hat z$ A·m².`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $\vec\tau=0.018\hat z\times(0.20\hat x+0.40\hat z)=0.018(0.20)(\hat z\times\hat x)=3.6\times10^{-3}\,\hat y$ N·m. The loop turns about the $y$ axis in the right-hand sense about $+\hat y$, so $\vec\mu$ swings from $+\hat z$ toward $+\hat x$ to line up with $\vec B$. Equilibrium: $\tan^{-1}(0.2/0.4)=26.6°$ from $+z$ toward $+x$.`,
   R`<b>(c) Forces.</b> Sides counterclockwise: $\vec L_1=a\hat x$, $\vec L_2=b\hat y$, $\vec L_3=-a\hat x$, $\vec L_4=-b\hat y$. $\vec F_1=Ia\hat x\times\vec B=-IaB_z\hat y=-0.12\hat y$ N, $\vec F_3=+0.12\hat y$ N. $\vec F_2=Ib\hat y\times(B_x\hat x+B_z\hat z)=Ib(B_z\hat x-B_x\hat z)=0.072\hat x-0.036\hat z$ N, $\vec F_4=-0.072\hat x+0.036\hat z$ N. Sum $=0$ ✓.`,
   R`<b>Torques about the centre</b> $(a/2,b/2,0)$: $\vec F_1,\vec F_3$ act at $\mp\tfrac b2\hat y$, parallel to their lever arms ⇒ no torque. $\vec r_2\times\vec F_2=\tfrac a2\hat x\times(-0.036\hat z)=+1.8\times10^{-3}\hat y$; $\vec r_4\times\vec F_4=-\tfrac a2\hat x\times(0.036\hat z)=+1.8\times10^{-3}\hat y$. Total $3.6\times10^{-3}\hat y$ N·m ✓ (the $z$-forces on sides 2 and 4 form a couple).`],
   R`(a) $\vec\mu=\mathbf{0.018\,\hat z}$ A·m². (b) $\vec\tau=\mathbf{3.6\times10^{-3}\,\hat y}$ N·m, rotation about $y$, $\vec\mu$ tipping toward $+x$. (c) Net force 0; side torques sum to the same $\vec\tau$.`)},
 {pid:"a4o:4", src:"HW4 Optional Q5 · two arcs", q:R`The closed loop in Figure OQ5 lies in the plane of the page. It consists of two concentric circular arcs of radii $R_1=3.0$ cm and $R_2=6.0$ cm, each subtending an angle $\phi_0=120°$ at the common centre O, joined by two radial straight segments. The loop carries $I=4.5$ A in the sense shown.<br>(a) Explain why the two radial segments contribute nothing to the field at O.<br>(b) Starting from the Biot–Savart law, show that a circular arc of radius $R$ subtending an angle $\phi_0$, measured in radians, produces $B=\mu_0I\phi_0/(4\pi R)$ at its centre.<br>(c) Find the magnitude and direction of the total $B$ at O.`,
  a:S(R`Biot–Savart: $d\vec B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\dfrac{d\vec l\times\hat r}{r^2}$. Radial pieces have $d\vec l\parallel\hat r$; arc pieces have $d\vec l\perp\hat r$ at fixed $r=R$.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> On a radial segment $d\vec l$ points along the line to O, so $d\vec l\times\hat r=0$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> On the arc $d\vec l=R\,d\phi\,\hat\phi\perp\hat r$, so $dB=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\dfrac{R\,d\phi}{R^2}$ and $B=\displaystyle\int_0^{\phi_0}\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi R}d\phi=\frac{\mu_0I\phi_0}{4\pi R}$. A full circle ($\phi_0=2\pi$) gives $\mu_0I/2R$ ✓.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> The inner arc runs counterclockwise (field out of the page); the outer arc runs clockwise (into the page). $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I\phi_0}{4\pi}\Big(\dfrac1{R_1}-\dfrac1{R_2}\Big)=10^{-7}(4.5)\Big(\dfrac{2\pi}{3}\Big)\Big(\dfrac1{0.030}-\dfrac1{0.060}\Big)=1.57\times10^{-5}$ T.`],
   R`$B=\mathbf{15.7\ \mu T}$ ($5\pi\ \mu$T), <b>out of the page</b> — the inner arc wins because it is closer.`,
   R`$\phi_0$ must be in radians: $120°=2\pi/3$.`)},
]},

{id:"hw5", title:"Homework 5 · Biot–Savart, Ampère's law, magnetic media", icon:"5️⃣", c:["#0ea5e9","#6366f1"], note:"Lectures 7–9. The seven main questions with detailed solutions; optional practice follows.", items:[
 {pid:"a5:0", src:"HW5 Q1 · wire with a semicircular bump", q:R`An infinitely long straight wire carries a current $I$. One segment of the wire is bent into a semicircle of radius $R$, as shown in Figure Q1. Calculate the magnetic flux density $B$ at the center O of the semicircle.`,
  a:S(R`Split the wire into the two straight halves and the semicircle, and add their Biot–Savart contributions.`,[
   R`<b>Straight parts.</b> Both straight pieces lie on a line through O, so on them $d\vec l\parallel\hat R$ and $d\vec l\times\hat R=0$: no field at O.`,
   R`<b>Semicircle.</b> Every element is at distance $R$ and perpendicular to $\hat R$: $B=\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi}\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\frac{R\,d\phi}{R^2}=\frac{\mu_0I}{4R}$ (half of a full loop's $\mu_0I/2R$).`,
   R`<b>Direction.</b> The current goes left to right over the top of O, i.e. clockwise as seen in the figure. By the right-hand rule the field at O points into the page.`],
   R`$B=\mathbf{\dfrac{\mu_0I}{4R}}$, <b>into the page</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a5:1", src:"HW5 Q2 · Helmholtz coil", q:R`Two identical flat circular coils, each with $N=100$ turns and radius $R=15$ cm, share a common axis (the $z$-axis), carry the same current $I=1.5$ A in the same sense, and are separated by a distance equal to their radius $R$, as shown in Figure Q2. This arrangement is called a Helmholtz coil. Take $z=0$ at the midpoint between the coils.<br>(a) Write an expression for $B_z(z)$ on the axis.<br>(b) Evaluate $B$ at the midpoint $z=0$.<br>(c) Evaluate $B$ at $z=R/4$ and at $z=R/2$ (the plane of coil 2), and express each as a percentage change relative to $B(0)$. Comment on the uniformity of the field near the center.`,
  a:S(R`One loop on its axis: $B=\dfrac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+s^2)^{3/2}}$ at distance $s$ from its plane. Multiply by $N$ and superpose the two coils at $z=\mp R/2$.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $B_z(z)=\dfrac{\mu_0NIR^2}{2}\Big[\big(R^2+(z+\tfrac R2)^2\big)^{-3/2}+\big(R^2+(z-\tfrac R2)^2\big)^{-3/2}\Big]$.`,
   R`<b>Prefactor.</b> $\dfrac{\mu_0NI}{2R}=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(100)(1.5)}{2(0.15)}=6.283\times10^{-4}$ T. Then $B=6.283\times10^{-4}\,[\,u_1^{-3/2}+u_2^{-3/2}]$ with $u=1+(s/R)^2$.`,
   R`<b>(b) $z=0$:</b> both $s=R/2$, $u=1.25$, $1.25^{-3/2}=0.7155$. $B(0)=6.283\times10^{-4}(1.4311)=8.99\times10^{-4}$ T $=\dfrac{8\mu_0NI}{5\sqrt5\,R}$.`,
   R`<b>(c) $z=R/4$:</b> $s=3R/4$ and $R/4$: $1.5625^{-3/2}+1.0625^{-3/2}=0.5120+0.9131=1.4251$ ⇒ $B=8.95\times10^{-4}$ T, $-0.42\%$.<br><b>$z=R/2$:</b> $s=R$ and $0$: $2^{-3/2}+1=1.3536$ ⇒ $B=8.50\times10^{-4}$ T, $-5.4\%$.`,
   R`<b>Comment.</b> Moving a quarter of the radius off-centre changes $B$ by less than half a percent: with spacing $R$ both $dB/dz$ and $d^2B/dz^2$ vanish at the centre, which is why Helmholtz coils are used to make uniform fields.`],
   R`(a) above. (b) $B(0)=\mathbf{0.90\ mT}$. (c) $B(R/4)=0.895$ mT ($\mathbf{-0.4\%}$), $B(R/2)=0.850$ mT ($\mathbf{-5.4\%}$): very uniform near the centre.`)},
 {pid:"a5:2", src:"HW5 Q3 · coaxial cable (Ampère)", q:R`Consider a coaxial cable whose inner conductor has radius $a$, and whose outer conductor has inner radius $b$ and outer radius $c$, where $a<b<c$. A current $I$ flows into the page in the inner conductor and returns out of the page in the outer conductor. The current is uniformly distributed over the cross section of each conductor. Determine the magnetic field everywhere, including both its magnitude and direction, as a function of the distance $r$ from the center of the cable.`,
  a:S(R`Cylindrical symmetry ⇒ $\vec B=B(r)\hat\phi$. Ampère's law on a circle of radius $r$: $B\,2\pi r=\mu_0I_{enc}(r)$. Only $I_{enc}$ changes between regions.`,[
   R`<b>$r<a$:</b> $I_{enc}=I\dfrac{\pi r^2}{\pi a^2}$ ⇒ $B=\dfrac{\mu_0Ir}{2\pi a^2}$.`,
   R`<b>$a<r<b$:</b> $I_{enc}=I$ ⇒ $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r}$.`,
   R`<b>$b<r<c$:</b> the loop also encloses the part of the return current inside $r$: $I_{enc}=I-I\dfrac{r^2-b^2}{c^2-b^2}=I\dfrac{c^2-r^2}{c^2-b^2}$ ⇒ $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r}\dfrac{c^2-r^2}{c^2-b^2}$.`,
   R`<b>$r>c$:</b> $I_{enc}=I-I=0$ ⇒ $B=0$ (the cable produces no external field).`,
   R`<b>Direction.</b> Net enclosed current is into the page, so by the right-hand rule the field circulates <b>clockwise</b> as seen in the figure.`],
   R`$B=\dfrac{\mu_0Ir}{2\pi a^2}$ ($r<a$); $\dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r}$ ($a<r<b$); $\dfrac{\mu_0I(c^2-r^2)}{2\pi r(c^2-b^2)}$ ($b<r<c$); $0$ ($r>c$); circulating clockwise.`)},
 {pid:"a5:3", src:"HW5 Q4 · conductor with an off-centre hole", q:R`A conductor has a cylindrical cross section of radius $a=2$ mm. A nonconducting cylindrical cut-out of radius $b=0.5$ mm is made inside the conductor. The distance between the center of the conductor and the center of the cut-out is $d=1$ mm. The current density in the conductor has magnitude $J=10^5$ A/m². Determine the magnitude of the magnetic field at point P, which is a distance $r=0.5$ mm from the center of the conductor along the line joining the two centers. [Hint: Use the superposition principle.]`,
  a:S(R`Superposition: full solid cylinder carrying $+J$ plus a small cylinder carrying $-J$ where the hole is. Inside a uniform cylinder, Ampère gives $B=\mu_0J\rho/2$, i.e. $\vec B=\tfrac{\mu_0}{2}\vec J\times\vec\rho$ with $\vec\rho$ measured from that cylinder's axis.`,[
   R`<b>Geometry.</b> Put O at the origin and the hole centre at $x=d=1$ mm. P is at $x=0.5$ mm: $0.5$ mm from O and $0.5$ mm $=b$ from the hole centre (on the hole's edge).`,
   R`<b>Full cylinder (+J):</b> $B_1=\dfrac{\mu_0Jr}{2}=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(10^5)(0.5\times10^{-3})}{2}=3.14\times10^{-5}$ T, along $\hat z\times\hat x=+\hat y$ (current out of page).`,
   R`<b>Hole (−J):</b> P is $0.5$ mm on the $-x$ side of the hole axis, so $\vec B_2=\tfrac{\mu_0}{2}(-J\hat z)\times(-0.5\text{ mm}\,\hat x)=+\dfrac{\mu_0J(0.5\text{ mm})}{2}\hat y=3.14\times10^{-5}$ T along $+\hat y$.`,
   R`<b>Total:</b> $B=6.28\times10^{-5}$ T. This equals $\dfrac{\mu_0Jd}{2}$ — the field is uniform across the whole hole, and P sits on its edge.`],
   R`$B=\dfrac{\mu_0Jd}{2}=\mathbf{6.28\times10^{-5}\ T}$ ($62.8\ \mu$T).`)},
 {pid:"a5:4", src:"HW5 Q5 · force between parallel wires", q:R`Consider two infinitely long, straight, freestanding, parallel wires separated by a distance $d$. The wires carry currents $I_1$ and $I_2$ in the positive $z$ direction and are located at $y=-d/2$ and $y=d/2$, respectively. Determine the force per unit length experienced by each wire, including its direction.`,
  a:S(R`Find the field of wire 1 at wire 2 and use $\vec F=I\vec L\times\vec B$; Newton's third law gives the other force.`,[
   R`<b>Field of wire 1 at wire 2.</b> Wire 2 is at $+\hat y$ from wire 1, a distance $d$ away. $\hat\phi=\hat z\times\hat y=-\hat x$, so $\vec B_1=-\dfrac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi d}\hat x$.`,
   R`<b>Force on wire 2.</b> $\vec F_2'=I_2\hat z\times\vec B_1=-\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}(\hat z\times\hat x)=-\hat y\,\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}$: toward wire 1.`,
   R`<b>Force on wire 1.</b> By symmetry (or Newton's third law) $\vec F_1'=+\hat y\,\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}$: toward wire 2.`],
   R`$\vec F_1'=+\hat y\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}$, $\vec F_2'=-\hat y\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}$ N/m: <b>parallel currents attract</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a5:5", src:"HW5 Q6 · three magnetic media", q:R`Three magnetic media with parallel interfaces are shown in Figure Q6. By applying the magnetic boundary conditions at both interfaces, show that, if no surface current densities exist at the interfaces, the relationship between $\theta_4$ and $\theta_1$ is independent of $\mu_2$.`,
  a:S(R`At each interface: $B_{1n}=B_{2n}$ and (with no surface current) $H_{1t}=H_{2t}$. Angles are measured from the normal.`,[
   R`<b>Interface 1–2.</b> Normal: $B_1\cos\theta_1=B_2\cos\theta_2$. Tangential: $\dfrac{B_1\sin\theta_1}{\mu_1}=\dfrac{B_2\sin\theta_2}{\mu_2}$. Dividing: $\dfrac{\tan\theta_1}{\mu_1}=\dfrac{\tan\theta_2}{\mu_2}$, so $\tan\theta_2=\dfrac{\mu_2}{\mu_1}\tan\theta_1$.`,
   R`<b>Inside medium 2</b> the field line is straight and the interfaces are parallel, so it meets the second interface at the same angle: $\theta_3=\theta_2$.`,
   R`<b>Interface 2–3.</b> Same algebra: $\tan\theta_4=\dfrac{\mu_3}{\mu_2}\tan\theta_3$.`,
   R`<b>Combine:</b> $\tan\theta_4=\dfrac{\mu_3}{\mu_2}\cdot\dfrac{\mu_2}{\mu_1}\tan\theta_1=\dfrac{\mu_3}{\mu_1}\tan\theta_1$. $\mu_2$ cancels.`],
   R`$\tan\theta_4=\dfrac{\mu_3}{\mu_1}\tan\theta_1$, <b>independent of</b> $\mu_2$.`)},
 {pid:"a5:6", src:"HW5 Q7 · C-core electromagnet", q:R`A C-shaped iron electromagnet is formed from a core of permeability $\mu\gg\mu_0$ and is wound with $N$ turns carrying a current $i$, as shown in Figure Q7. The cross-sectional area of the iron is $A$, the air gap has width $d$, and each side of the C-shaped mean magnetic path has length $\ell$. Neglecting magnetic leakage, determine the magnetic flux density $B$ in the air gap. [Hint: Use Ampère's law around the magnetic circuit.]`,
  a:S(R`Ampère's law for $\vec H$ around the mean path: $\oint\vec H\cdot d\vec l=Ni$. With no leakage the same flux $\Phi=BA$ crosses the iron and the gap, so $B$ is the same in both (normal $B$ is continuous at the gap faces).`,[
   R`<b>Path lengths.</b> The square mean path has four sides of length $\ell$; the gap takes $d$ out of it, so the iron path is $4\ell-d$.`,
   R`<b>H in each part.</b> Iron: $H_i=\dfrac{B}{\mu}$. Gap: $H_g=\dfrac{B}{\mu_0}$.`,
   R`<b>Ampère:</b> $\dfrac{B}{\mu}(4\ell-d)+\dfrac{B}{\mu_0}d=Ni$ ⇒ $B=\dfrac{Ni}{\dfrac{4\ell-d}{\mu}+\dfrac{d}{\mu_0}}$. In reluctance language $\mathcal R=\dfrac{4\ell-d}{\mu A}+\dfrac{d}{\mu_0A}$ and $\Phi=Ni/\mathcal R$.`,
   R`<b>$\mu\gg\mu_0$:</b> the iron term is negligible unless $4\ell/\mu$ is comparable to $d/\mu_0$, so $B\approx\dfrac{\mu_0Ni}{d}$. Almost all of the mmf is "spent" across the thin air gap.`],
   R`$B=\dfrac{\mu_0\mu Ni}{\mu_0(4\ell-d)+\mu d}\approx\mathbf{\dfrac{\mu_0Ni}{d}}$.`,
   R`If your figure counts the mean path differently, just replace $4\ell-d$ by the iron path length; the gap term is what dominates.`)},
]},

{id:"hw5o", title:"Homework 5 · optional practice (with solutions)", icon:"🧭", c:["#06b6d4","#8b5cf6"], note:"The supplied solutions, with the reasoning spelled out.", items:[
 {pid:"a5o:0", src:"HW5 Optional Q1 · slab in a normal H field", q:R`A uniform magnetic field $\vec H_1=H_1\vec a_z$ exists in free space. A very large slab lies perpendicular to this magnetic field, as shown in Figure O1. Ignoring edge effects:<br>(a) If the slab material has permeability $\mu$, determine the magnetic field intensity in the slab.<br>(b) If the slab is a permanent magnet with magnetization $\vec M_i=M_i\vec a_z$, determine the magnetic field intensity in the slab.`,
  a:S(R`The field is normal to the faces, so the useful condition is $B_{1n}=B_{2n}$.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $\mu_0H_1=\mu H_2$ ⇒ $\vec H_2=\dfrac{\mu_0}{\mu}H_1\vec a_z$. $\vec B$ is unchanged ($\mu_0H_1$) but $\vec H$ is reduced by $\mu_r$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> In a permanent magnet $\vec B_2=\mu_0(\vec H_2+\vec M_i)$. $B_{1n}=B_{2n}$: $\mu_0H_1=\mu_0(H_2+M_i)$ ⇒ $\vec H_2=(H_1-M_i)\vec a_z$.`],
   R`(a) $\vec H_2=\mathbf{\dfrac{\mu_0}{\mu}H_1\,a_z}$. (b) $\vec H_2=\mathbf{(H_1-M_i)\,a_z}$.`)},
 {pid:"a5o:1", src:"HW5 Optional Q2 · square loop under a wire", q:R`A square loop with side length $a=2$ m carries a current $I_1=5$ A. An infinitely long straight conductor carrying a current $I_2=10$ A is placed parallel to one pair of the loop's sides and just above the midpoints of the other pair, as shown in Figure O2. Determine the net magnetic force acting on the loop.`,
  a:S(R`Treat the wire as lying in the loop's plane, along the loop's centre line. Sides 1 and 3 are parallel to the wire at distance $a/2$; sides 2 and 4 cross it.`,[
   R`<b>Side 1</b> (current parallel to $I_2$, attracted): $F_1=\dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2a}{2\pi(a/2)}=\dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(5)(10)(2)}{2\pi(1)}=2\times10^{-5}$ N along $+\hat y$ (toward the wire).`,
   R`<b>Side 3</b> (antiparallel, repelled from the wire): it is on the other side, so "away from the wire" is also $+\hat y$. $F_3=F_1$.`,
   R`<b>Sides 2 and 4</b> cross the wire; the field reverses sign at the wire, so the forces on their two halves cancel.`],
   R`$\vec F=2F_1=\mathbf{4\times10^{-5}\,\hat y}$ <b>N</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a5o:2", src:"HW5 Optional Q3 · magnetic refraction", q:R`The $x$-$y$ plane separates two magnetic media with permeabilities $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$, as shown in Figure O3. If there is no surface current at the interface and the magnetic field in medium 1 is $\vec H_1=\hat xH_{1x}+\hat yH_{1y}+\hat zH_{1z}$, find: (a) $\vec H_2$; (b) the angles $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$; (c) the numerical values of $\vec H_2$, $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$ for $H_{1x}=2$ A/m, $H_{1y}=0$, $H_{1z}=4$ A/m, $\mu_1=\mu_0$ and $\mu_2=4\mu_0$.`,
  a:S(R`Normal is $\hat z$. Tangential $H$ is continuous (no $J_s$); normal $B$ is continuous.`,[
   R`<b>(a)</b> $H_{2x}=H_{1x}$, $H_{2y}=H_{1y}$, $\mu_2H_{2z}=\mu_1H_{1z}$ ⇒ $\vec H_2=\hat xH_{1x}+\hat yH_{1y}+\hat z\dfrac{\mu_1}{\mu_2}H_{1z}$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> From the normal: $\tan\theta_1=\dfrac{\sqrt{H_{1x}^2+H_{1y}^2}}{H_{1z}}$, $\tan\theta_2=\dfrac{\mu_2}{\mu_1}\tan\theta_1$.`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> $\vec H_2=2\hat x+\tfrac14(4)\hat z=2\hat x+\hat z$ A/m. $\theta_1=\tan^{-1}(2/4)=26.6°$, $\theta_2=\tan^{-1}(2/1)=63.4°$.`],
   R`(c) $\vec H_2=\mathbf{2\hat x+\hat z}$ A/m, $\theta_1=\mathbf{26.6°}$, $\theta_2=\mathbf{63.4°}$. The field bends away from the normal going into the higher-$\mu$ medium.`)},
 {pid:"a5o:3", src:"HW5 Optional Q4 · hollow conductor (superposition)", q:R`An infinitely long conducting cylinder of radius $3a$ contains a cylindrical hole of radius $a$, as shown in Figure O4. The axis of the hole is parallel to the cylinder axis and is a distance $a$ from it. A current $I$ flows out of the page and is uniformly distributed over the conducting cross section.<br>(a) Determine the magnetic field intensity $\vec H$ at any point on the plane P (plane $y=0$).<br>(b) Show that the magnetic field is uniform everywhere inside the hole, and determine its value.`,
  a:S(R`$J=\dfrac{I}{\pi(3a)^2-\pi a^2}=\dfrac{I}{8\pi a^2}$. Field = (solid cylinder radius $3a$ with $+J$) $-$ (solid cylinder radius $a$ at $(a,0)$ with $+J$). For a uniform cylinder of radius $R$: inside $\vec H=\tfrac J2(-y\hat x+x\hat y)$, outside $\vec H=\dfrac{JR^2}{2\rho^2}(-y\hat x+x\hat y)$, with $(x,y)$ measured from its axis.`,[
   R`<b>Big cylinder</b> ($R=3a$, $I_2=J\pi(3a)^2=\tfrac98I$): $\vec H_2=\dfrac{I}{16\pi a^2}(-y\hat x+x\hat y)$ for $r\le3a$; $\dfrac{9I}{16\pi(x^2+y^2)}(-y\hat x+x\hat y)$ for $r>3a$.`,
   R`<b>Hole cylinder</b> ($R=a$, centre $(a,0)$, $I_1=\tfrac I8$): $\vec H_1=\dfrac{I}{16\pi a^2}(-y\hat x+(x-a)\hat y)$ inside; $\dfrac{I}{16\pi[(x-a)^2+y^2]}(-y\hat x+(x-a)\hat y)$ outside. Total $\vec H=\vec H_2-\vec H_1$.`,
   R`<b>(a) On $y=0$</b> every $\hat x$ term vanishes, so $\vec H=H_y\hat y$:<br>• inside the hole $0<x<2a$: $H_y=\dfrac{x}{16\pi a^2}I-\dfrac{x-a}{16\pi a^2}I=\dfrac{I}{16\pi a}$;<br>• in the metal ($2a\le x\le3a$ or $-3a\le x\le0$): $H_y=\dfrac{Ix}{16\pi a^2}-\dfrac{I}{16\pi(x-a)}=\dfrac{I(x^2-ax-a^2)}{16\pi a^2(x-a)}$;<br>• outside ($|x|>3a$): $H_y=\dfrac{9I}{16\pi x}-\dfrac{I}{16\pi(x-a)}=\dfrac{I(8x-9a)}{16\pi x(x-a)}$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> Any point in the hole is inside both cylinders, so both "inside" formulas apply: $H_x=-\dfrac{Iy}{16\pi a^2}+\dfrac{Iy}{16\pi a^2}=0$, $H_y=\dfrac{I[x-(x-a)]}{16\pi a^2}=\dfrac{I}{16\pi a}$ — no $x$ or $y$ left, so it is uniform.`],
   R`(a) as above. (b) $\vec H_{hole}=\mathbf{\dfrac{I}{16\pi a}\,\hat y}$, uniform.`)},
]},

{id:"hw6", title:"Homework 6 · magnetic materials, Faraday's law, inductance", icon:"6️⃣", c:["#f97316","#ef4444"], note:"Lectures 9–10e (due 26 Nov). The five main questions with detailed solutions; optional practice follows.", items:[
 {pid:"a6:0", src:"HW6 Q1 · hysteresis loop (MCQ)", q:R`The figure shows the magnetization ($M$) versus the applied external magnetic field ($B_0$) curve for a certain material. Based on the features of this curve, which of the following statements is CORRECT?<br>A. The horizontal axis $B_0$ represents the external magnetic field, and point $b$ represents the residual magnetization (remanence) that remains after the external field is removed.<br>B. Point $c$ represents the saturation magnetization where all magnetic dipoles are perfectly aligned with the external field.<br>C. The closed loop shape indicates that the material is a typical paramagnetic material, which has a linear response to the external field.<br>D. Point $a$ represents the state where the material has completely lost its magnetism because the external field is too strong.`,
  a:S(R`Read each labelled point of a hysteresis loop: $a$ top corner, $b$ on the $M$ axis ($B_0=0$), $c$ on the $B_0$ axis ($M=0$).`,[
   R`<b>A — correct.</b> At $b$ the applied field is zero but $M>0$: that is the remanence.`,
   R`<b>B — wrong.</b> At $c$, $M=0$: that is the coercive field (the reverse field needed to demagnetize), not saturation.`,
   R`<b>C — wrong.</b> A loop (history dependence) is the signature of a ferromagnet. Paramagnets have a single straight line through the origin.`,
   R`<b>D — wrong.</b> Point $a$ is saturation: the material is fully magnetized, not demagnetized.`],
   R`<b>A</b>.`)},
 {pid:"a6:1", src:"HW6 Q2 · loop in a time-varying B", q:R`As shown in Figure 1, a circular conducting loop with a radius $\rho=15$ cm has a resistor $R=250\ \Omega$ connected across its ends (node $a$ on top, node $b$ on bottom). A uniform time-varying magnetic field $B=0.2\cos(120\pi t)$ T points out of the page. The connecting wires are assumed to be perfectly conducting, and the magnetic field generated by the loop current $I(t)$ is negligible. Determine: (a) the voltage $V_{ab}(t)$; (b) the current $I(t)$.`,
  a:S(R`Faraday: $\mathcal E=-\dfrac{d\Phi}{dt}$ around the loop, with the positive sense set by the right-hand rule on the normal (out of page ⇒ counterclockwise). All of the emf appears across the resistor because the wire has no resistance.`,[
   R`<b>Flux</b> (normal out of page): $\Phi=B\pi\rho^2=0.2\pi(0.15)^2\cos(120\pi t)=1.414\times10^{-2}\cos(120\pi t)$ Wb.`,
   R`<b>emf</b> (counterclockwise positive): $\mathcal E=-\dfrac{d\Phi}{dt}=1.414\times10^{-2}(120\pi)\sin(120\pi t)=5.33\sin(120\pi t)$ V.`,
   R`<b>Lenz check.</b> Just after $t=0$, $B$ (out) is decreasing, so the induced current flows counterclockwise to keep the outward flux up. On the right-hand side of the loop counterclockwise is upward, so the current goes through $R$ from $b$ up to $a$: $b$ is the higher-potential end.`,
   R`<b>(a)</b> Current enters the resistor at $b$, so $V_{ab}=V_a-V_b=-\mathcal E=-5.33\sin(120\pi t)$ V.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> With $I(t)$ taken counterclockwise as drawn: $I=\dfrac{\mathcal E}{R}=\dfrac{5.33}{250}\sin(120\pi t)=21.3\sin(120\pi t)$ mA.`],
   R`(a) $V_{ab}=\mathbf{-5.33\sin(120\pi t)}$ <b>V</b> (amplitude 5.33 V). (b) $I=\mathbf{21.3\sin(120\pi t)}$ <b>mA</b>, counterclockwise when positive.`,
   R`The overall sign depends on how $a$, $b$ and $I$ are oriented in the figure; get it from Lenz's law, not from memory. $120\pi=377$ rad/s.`)},
 {pid:"a6:2", src:"HW6 Q3 · AC generator (= May 2022 P4)", q:R`A simple electric generator rotates with frequency $f$ about the $y$-axis in a uniform $\vec B$ field. The rotor consists of $n$ windings of area $S$. It powers a lightbulb of resistance $R$ (all other wires have no resistance).<br>(a) What is the maximum value $I_{max}$ of the induced current? What is the orientation of the coil when this current is achieved?<br>(b) What power must be supplied to maintain the rotation (ignoring friction)?`,
  a:S(R`The angle between $\vec B$ and the coil normal grows as $\theta=\omega t$, $\omega=2\pi f$, so the flux through each winding is $BS\cos\omega t$.`,[
   R`<b>emf.</b> $\mathcal E=-n\dfrac{d}{dt}(BS\cos\omega t)=nBS\omega\sin\omega t=2\pi fnBS\sin(2\pi ft)$.`,
   R`<b>(a)</b> $I=\dfrac{\mathcal E}{R}$, so $I_{max}=\dfrac{2\pi fnBS}{R}$. It happens when $|\sin\omega t|=1$, i.e. the coil normal is perpendicular to $\vec B$ — the plane of the coil is <b>parallel</b> to $\vec B$. The flux is zero there but changing fastest.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> Energy conservation: the mechanical power supplied equals the power dissipated in the bulb. $P(t)=I^2R=\dfrac{(2\pi fnBS)^2}{R}\sin^2(2\pi ft)$. Averaged over a cycle ($\langle\sin^2\rangle=\tfrac12$): $\langle P\rangle=\dfrac{(2\pi fnBS)^2}{2R}=\dfrac{2\pi^2f^2n^2B^2S^2}{R}$.`,
   R`<b>Why work is needed.</b> The induced current feels $\vec F=I\vec L\times\vec B$, which produces a torque $\tau=nISB\sin\omega t$ opposing the rotation (Lenz). The applied torque must match it: $P=\tau\omega=I^2R$ ✓.`],
   R`(a) $I_{max}=\mathbf{\dfrac{2\pi fnBS}{R}}$, coil plane parallel to $\vec B$. (b) $P(t)=\dfrac{(2\pi fnBS)^2}{R}\sin^2(2\pi ft)$, average $\mathbf{\dfrac{(2\pi fnBS)^2}{2R}}$.`)},
 {pid:"a6:3", src:"HW6 Q4 · two attached solenoids (= Final 2023 B1)", q:R`The inductor consists of two solenoids (radius $b$, $n$ turns/m, and radius $a$, $3n$ turns/m) attached together such that the current pictured goes counterclockwise in both of them according to the observer. What is the self-inductance of the inductor?`,
  a:S(R`The two solenoids are coaxial and the same length $\ell$, with the radius-$a$ one ($3n$ turns/m) inside the radius-$b$ one ($n$ turns/m), carrying the same current $I$ in the same sense. Find $B$ in each region, then add up the flux linkage of every turn: $L=\dfrac{\Lambda}{I}$.`,
  [R`<b>Fields</b> (ideal solenoids, $B=\mu_0nI$ inside each, zero outside, superposed): for $r<a$ both contribute, $B=\mu_0(3n+n)I=4\mu_0nI$; for $a<r<b$ only the outer one, $B=\mu_0nI$.`,
   R`<b>Inner turns</b> ($3n\ell$ of them, radius $a$): each links $\Phi_a=4\mu_0nI\cdot\pi a^2$. Linkage $3n\ell\cdot4\pi\mu_0nIa^2=12\pi\mu_0n^2\ell Ia^2$.`,
   R`<b>Outer turns</b> ($n\ell$ of them, radius $b$): each links $4\mu_0nI\pi a^2+\mu_0nI\pi(b^2-a^2)=\pi\mu_0nI(b^2+3a^2)$. Linkage $\pi\mu_0n^2\ell I(b^2+3a^2)$.`,
   R`<b>Total:</b> $\Lambda=\pi\mu_0n^2\ell I(12a^2+b^2+3a^2)=\pi\mu_0n^2\ell I(b^2+15a^2)$, so $L=\Lambda/I$.`],
   R`$L=\mathbf{\mu_0\pi n^2\ell\,(b^2+15a^2)}$.`,
   R`Check: with no inner solenoid ($a=0$) this is the single-solenoid result $\mu_0n^2\ell\pi b^2$. The $15a^2$ is $12a^2$ (inner self-linkage, $3n\times4n$) plus $3a^2$ (outer turns picking up the inner solenoid's extra field, $n\times3n$).`)},
 {pid:"a6:4", src:"HW6 Q5 · wire–toroid transformer", q:R`The transformer shown in the figure consists of a long wire coincident with the $z$-axis carrying a current $I=I_0\cos\omega t$, coupling magnetic energy to a toroidal coil situated in the $x$-$y$ plane and centered at the origin. The toroidal core uses iron material with relative permeability $\mu_r$, around which 100 turns of a tightly wound coil serve to induce a voltage $V_{emf}$, as shown in the figure.<br>(a) Develop an expression for $V_{emf}$.<br>(b) Calculate $V_{emf}$ for $f=60$ Hz, $\mu_r=4000$, $a=5$ cm, $b=6$ cm, $c=2$ cm, and $I_0=50$ A.`,
  a:S(R`The wire's field inside the core is $B=\dfrac{\mu I}{2\pi r}$ (Ampère with $\mu=\mu_r\mu_0$). The core cross-section is a rectangle $a<r<b$, height $c$. Faraday with $N=100$ turns.`,[
   R`<b>Flux through the core cross-section:</b> $\Phi=\displaystyle\int_a^b\frac{\mu I}{2\pi r}c\,dr=\frac{\mu cI}{2\pi}\ln\frac ba$.`,
   R`<b>(a)</b> $V_{emf}=-N\dfrac{d\Phi}{dt}=-\dfrac{N\mu c}{2\pi}\ln\dfrac ba\dfrac{d}{dt}(I_0\cos\omega t)=\dfrac{N\mu_r\mu_0c\,\omega I_0}{2\pi}\ln\Big(\dfrac ba\Big)\sin\omega t$.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $\mu=4000(4\pi\times10^{-7})=5.027\times10^{-3}$ H/m, $\omega=2\pi(60)=377$ rad/s, $\ln(6/5)=0.1823$.<br>$V_0=\dfrac{100(5.027\times10^{-3})(0.02)(377)(50)}{2\pi}(0.1823)=5.50$ V.`],
   R`(a) $V_{emf}=\mathbf{\dfrac{N\mu c\,\omega I_0}{2\pi}\ln\dfrac ba\,\sin\omega t}$. (b) $V_{emf}=\mathbf{5.50\sin(377t)}$ <b>V</b>.`,
   R`The sign of $V_{emf}$ depends on the winding sense in the figure; the amplitude 5.5 V does not.`)},
]},

{id:"hw6o", title:"Homework 6 · optional practice (with solutions)", icon:"⚡", c:["#ef4444","#f59e0b"], note:"The supplied solutions, with the reasoning spelled out.", items:[
 {pid:"a6o:0", src:"HW6 Optional Q6 · Faraday's law around a ring", q:R`A perfectly conducting filament is formed into a circular ring of radius $a$. At one point, a resistance $R$ is inserted into the circuit, and at another a battery of voltage $V_0$ is inserted. Assume that the loop current itself produces negligible magnetic field. Given Faraday's law $\oint\vec E\cdot d\vec l=-\dfrac{d}{dt}\int\vec B\cdot d\vec S=-\dfrac{d\Phi_B}{dt}$:<br>(a) Apply Faraday's law, evaluating each side of the equation carefully and independently to show the equality;<br>(b) repeat part (a), assuming the battery is removed, the ring is closed again, and a linearly increasing $\vec B$ field is applied in a direction normal to the loop surface.`,
  a:S(R`Evaluate $\oint\vec E\cdot d\vec l$ piece by piece around the ring (going with the current), and the flux term separately.`,[
   R`<b>(a) LHS.</b> In the perfect conductor $E=0$. Across the resistor $\int\vec E\cdot d\vec l=IR$. Through the battery (− to + in the direction of the current) the field points against the path: $-V_0$. So $\oint\vec E\cdot d\vec l=IR-V_0$.`,
   R`<b>(a) RHS.</b> No external field and negligible self-field ⇒ $\Phi_B=0$ ⇒ $-d\Phi_B/dt=0$. So $IR-V_0=0$: $IR=V_0$, which is Kirchhoff's voltage law.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> Let $\vec B=kt\,\vec a_n$. LHS: only the resistor contributes, $\oint\vec E\cdot d\vec l=IR$. RHS: $\Phi_B=kt\,\pi a^2$, $-d\Phi_B/dt=-k\pi a^2$. So $IR=-k\pi a^2$ ⇒ $I=-\dfrac{k\pi a^2}{R}$.`],
   R`(a) $IR-V_0=0$ ⇒ $\mathbf{I=V_0/R}$. (b) $\mathbf{I=-k\pi a^2/R}$; the minus sign (Lenz) means the current circulates so as to oppose the growing flux.`)},
 {pid:"a6o:1", src:"HW6 Optional Q7 · displacement current", q:R`Consider the region defined by $|x|,|y|,|z|<1$. Let $\epsilon_r=5$, $\mu_r=4$ and $\sigma=0$. If $\vec J_d=20\cos(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\,\vec a_y\ \mu$A/m²: (a) find $\vec D$ and $\vec E$; (b) if $\vec B=2b\times10^{-11}\sin(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\,\vec a_z$ T, use Ampère's law with Maxwell's extension to find $\vec J_d$; (c) what is the numerical value of $b$?`,
  a:S(R`$\vec J_d=\partial\vec D/\partial t$, so integrate in time for $\vec D$. With $\sigma=0$ there is no conduction current, so $\nabla\times\vec H=\vec J_d$.`,[
   R`<b>Constants.</b> $\epsilon=5\epsilon_0=\dfrac{5\times10^{-9}}{36\pi}$ F/m, $\mu=4\mu_0=16\pi\times10^{-7}$ H/m.`,
   R`<b>(a)</b> $\vec D=\displaystyle\int\vec J_d\,dt=\frac{20\times10^{-6}}{1.5\times10^8}\sin(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\vec a_y=1.33\times10^{-13}\sin(\cdot)\vec a_y$ C/m². $\vec E=\dfrac{\vec D}{\epsilon}=\dfrac{1.33\times10^{-13}(36\pi)}{5\times10^{-9}}\sin(\cdot)\vec a_y=3.02\times10^{-3}\sin(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\vec a_y$ V/m.`,
   R`<b>(b)</b> $\nabla\times\vec B=-\dfrac{\partial B_z}{\partial x}\vec a_y=2b^2\times10^{-11}\cos(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\vec a_y$. $\vec J_d=\dfrac1\mu\nabla\times\vec B=\dfrac{2b^2\times10^{-11}}{16\pi\times10^{-7}}\cos(\cdot)\vec a_y=\dfrac{b^2}{8\pi}\times10^{-4}\cos(1.5\times10^8t-bx)\vec a_y$ A/m².`,
   R`<b>(c)</b> Match amplitudes: $20\times10^{-6}=\dfrac{b^2}{8\pi}\times10^{-4}$ ⇒ $b^2=1.6\pi$ ⇒ $b=2.24$ rad/m.`],
   R`(a) $\vec D=\mathbf{1.33\times10^{-13}\sin(\cdot)\,a_y}$ C/m², $\vec E=\mathbf{3.02\times10^{-3}\sin(\cdot)\,a_y}$ V/m. (b) $\vec J_d=\dfrac{b^2}{8\pi}\times10^{-4}\cos(\cdot)\vec a_y$. (c) $b=\mathbf{2.24}$ <b>rad/m</b>.`,
   R`Check: $b=\omega\sqrt{\mu\epsilon}=1.5\times10^8\cdot\sqrt{20}/c=2.24$ ✓ — it is the wavenumber of a wave in this medium.`)},
 {pid:"a6o:2", src:"HW6 Optional Q8 · mutual inductance wire–loop", q:R`Determine the mutual inductance between the circular loop and the linear current shown in the figure. (The circular loop has radius $a$ and is centred at the origin; the linear current $I_1$ flows along the $x$ direction at $y=-d$, with $d>a$.)`,
  a:S(R`$M=\Phi_{12}/I_1$: find the flux of the wire's field through the loop by integrating over horizontal strips.`,[
   R`<b>Field</b> at height $y$ inside the loop (distance $d+y$ from the wire, out of the page): $B=\dfrac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi(d+y)}$.`,
   R`<b>Strip</b> at height $y$ has length $2\sqrt{a^2-y^2}$ and width $dy$: $\Phi_{12}=\displaystyle\int_{-a}^{a}\frac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi(d+y)}2\sqrt{a^2-y^2}\,dy=\frac{\mu_0I_1}{\pi}\int_{-a}^{a}\frac{\sqrt{a^2-y^2}}{y+d}dy$.`,
   R`<b>Standard integral</b> (substitute $y=a\sin\theta$): $\displaystyle\int_{-a}^{a}\frac{\sqrt{a^2-y^2}}{y+d}dy=\pi\big(d-\sqrt{d^2-a^2}\big)$. So $\Phi_{12}=\mu_0I_1\big(d-\sqrt{d^2-a^2}\big)$.`],
   R`$M=\mathbf{\mu_0\big(d-\sqrt{d^2-a^2}\big)}$.`,
   R`For $d\gg a$: $d-\sqrt{d^2-a^2}\approx\dfrac{a^2}{2d}$, so $M\approx\dfrac{\mu_0\pi a^2}{2\pi d}$ = (uniform field at the centre) × (loop area) ✓.`)},
]},
);
